# The Raze base OS's Buildroot options: the `os` of raze-base. Gaia starts from Buildroot's allnoconfig, so
# EVERYTHING here is on because a line says so. Each decision is written with its reason. Kernel decisions
# are in boards/raze/kernel/; the firmware pin is in families/bcm2712/family.toml.
# F8?: the Buildroot option names below are the ones the fragment form takes; Gaia's option check (the
# coming "every requested line survives") is the test. Anything it drops is a real finding.

# -- Init and the platform ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# systemd 258.7 (Buildroot 2026.08's version, checked in its package/systemd/systemd.mk): lemnosd's unit
# needs systemd 254+, and the Raze's units (lemnosd, lemnos-orion, Orion's comm and node) are systemd units.
BR2_INIT_SYSTEMD=y
BR2_PACKAGE_SYSTEMD=y
# 4 KiB pages: the page size dev measured on a Raze (also in the kernel fragment).
BR2_ARM64_PAGE_SIZE_4K=y
# The Pi firmware (1.20260915, the family's pin): the boot ROM files, start4.elf and the rest, so the Pi boots.
# The overlays the boot loads are the config.txt lines in boards/raze/boot/raze-device.txt (dtoverlay=vc4-kms-v3d-pi5
# and dtoverlay=ramoops-pi5); the kernel's overlays are installed by INSTALL_DTB_OVERLAYS below. There is no Buildroot
# option that names the overlay set: BR2_PACKAGE_RPI_FIRMWARE_DTB_OVERLAYS was refused at config-only by Gaia (PR B32:
# "no such option, or its dependencies are not met" at Buildroot 2026.08), so it is not written.
BR2_PACKAGE_RPI_FIRMWARE=y
BR2_PACKAGE_RPI_FIRMWARE_INSTALL_DTB_OVERLAYS=y

# -- The console and the way in -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# BusyBox is the image's one set of applets. Its config is os/busybox.config, a file beside this fragment (`@here@`
# is this directory; Gaia takes the file as an input tree and Buildroot reads it as BR2_PACKAGE_BUSYBOX_CONFIG).
# The applets it enables are the table in docs/prs/B40.md (39), changed for the board checks below (+5, -4), each with
# where the image calls it or why the
# recovery console needs it. Not enabled: every network server (telnetd, httpd, tftpd, inetd, dnsd, ftpd, udhcpd,
# ssl_server), the network clients but ip and ping (wget, tftp, nc, telnet, udhcpc), su, passwd, getty, vi, crond
# and init. login IS enabled: systemd's serial-getty@ttyGS0 runs util-linux's agetty, which execs /bin/login, and
# busybox login is the image's only one. It runs as root from agetty, so it needs no set-uid. No autologin.
# Orion's logins run /bin/sh (its comm core's default shell), so sh is kept.
# Set-uid: CONFIG_FEATURE_SUID is off. Buildroot 2026.08's busybox.mk nevertheless installs /bin/busybox mode 4755
# from its BUSYBOX_PERMISSIONS table in the single-binary build, whatever the config says. Not changed here: Gaia
# strips set-uid bits when it makes a slot root unless the product lists the file, and the next build's size report
# states whether the bit survived (docs/prs/B40.md).
BR2_PACKAGE_BUSYBOX=y
BR2_PACKAGE_BUSYBOX_CONFIG="@here@/busybox.config"
# /etc/profile without `export EDITOR=/bin/vi` (no editor on the board): the overlay directory's etc/profile is copied
# over the skeleton's after the install (Buildroot's BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY). No Buildroot option names EDITOR.
BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY="@here@/overlay"

# -- What the board checks run (Lemnos IMG-REQ-raze 10 and 28) ------------------------------------------
# Packages that replace or add to BusyBox applets are hidden in Buildroot unless this is set.
BR2_PACKAGE_BUSYBOX_SHOW_OTHERS=y
# kmod's tools: modprobe that honours modprobe.d's `blacklist` and `options` (lemnos.ko and the stock camera drivers
# it blacklists, IMG-REQ-raze 10 and 11), with modinfo, lsmod, insmod and rmmod. systemd already takes libkmod;
# the tools are kmod's one binary. BusyBox's insmod, lsmod, modprobe and rmmod are off in busybox.config so there is one of each.
BR2_PACKAGE_KMOD=y
BR2_PACKAGE_KMOD_TOOLS=y
# ps with -o and -C (the V1g check). BusyBox's ps has neither.
BR2_PACKAGE_PROCPS_NG=y
# gpioset and gpioget (the B15 check holds a GPIO line): libgpiod's tools. No BusyBox applet does this.
BR2_PACKAGE_LIBGPIOD2=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LIBGPIOD2_TOOLS=y
# flock (failsafe locks) and i2cdump, i2cget, i2cset and i2cdetect (the A35 check) are BusyBox applets
# (busybox.config), not util-linux's basic set (about 50 programs) or i2c-tools. No python3 and no compiler: with
# gpioset on the image, the checks need neither (IMG-REQ-raze 28).
# No sshd and no dropbear: Orion serves SSH itself (its comm core, ssh.listen), so a second server would be
# a second way in with its own keys.
# The console: a getty on the USB gadget's ACM port, ttyGS0, is systemd's serial-getty@ttyGS0.service. It is
# enabled in components/raze-base-files (a systemd unit, not a Buildroot option), because Buildroot's getty
# option is the sysvinit form. F8?: systemd's getty needs no Buildroot line.
# Console login follows the board's security setting, the one switch.
# Security OFF (the default for FRC): root logs in on this console with no
# password. It is the person's last way in when the network or Orion can't be
# reached, on a bench or a robot where physical access is already total.
# Security ON: console login must be off. How a running board switches this
# when security is turned on is not defined yet (OPEN); this fragment is the
# default.
BR2_TARGET_ENABLE_ROOT_LOGIN=y
BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_ROOT_PASSWD=""
# Kernel console decision (B13): OPEN, nothing masked. The kernel's console= comes from the image's own
# cmdline.txt (Gaia F10: "the image's own, then <P>.slot=..."). No file on next sets it: this fragment names no
# BR2_PACKAGE_RPI_FIRMWARE_CMDLINE_FILE, and no devices/ file has a console=. So the kernel console tty is not
# known from these files, and the getty above (ttyGS0) is not checked against it. Dev's value was
# console=serial0,115200 console=tty1 (dev families/rpi-bcm2712/gaia/slots.toml). If that is used, the kernel
# console is serial0 (dev's slot tests name it ttyAMA10 on this board), so systemd would add a second getty
# on it, serial-getty@ttyAMA10; tty1 would be a keyboard getty. Gaia has no mask form (a component's [[install]]
# copies files, [[services]] has enable only), so a mask needs Gaia's form first. Decide with the cmdline.

# -- Root filesystem ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read-only root: the slot's root is EROFS with dm-verity, which cannot be written. The writable place is
# the data partition (the slots and the state), not the root. So BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_REMOUNT_ROOTFS_RW stays OFF.

# -- Network ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# systemd-networkd is the network manager (systemd's own; no ifupdown). It STAYS on: BR2_PACKAGE_SYSTEMD_NETWORKD=y.
# Reasons: lemnos0-address (components/raze-base-files) writes /run/systemd/network/10-lemnos0.network for networkd;
# that file's DHCPServer is the Raze's DHCP to the host (the ruling below); and Orion's peer listen address is the
# board's address that file sets. Without networkd the board has no lemnos0 address and no DHCP server. networkd is
# Type=notify-reload with a BusName= it advertises: it starts without the bus, so post-build.sh does not refuse it.
# Buildroot defaults it to y; on Gaia's allnoconfig base it is off unless a fragment says so, hence this line.
BR2_PACKAGE_SYSTEMD_NETWORKD=y

# -- The bus daemons (PR B41) -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Off: systemd-resolved, systemd-timesyncd, systemd-hostnamed and systemd-timedated. Reasons:
#   - nothing on the image uses the bus (the scan of the built root: libdbus-1 is linked only by dbus's own tools;
#     no Lemnos or Orion binary uses the bus);
#   - resolved: the image's name lookups are nsswitch's `resolve [!UNAVAIL=return] files myhostname dns`, which falls
#     through when resolved is absent;
#   - timesyncd: the clock comes from Orion's time sync (parked), not from timesyncd;
#   - hostnamed and timedated: the hostname and the clock timezone are not set through the bus on this image.
# Buildroot defaults each of the four to y; the fragment sets them off so the fragment states it. dbus itself cannot be
# turned off by a line here: package/systemd/Config.in selects it (`select BR2_PACKAGE_DBUS if !BR2_PACKAGE_DBUS_BROKER`).
# os/post-build.sh removes dbus from the installed tree and fails the build if anything still needs it.
# BR2_PACKAGE_SYSTEMD_RESOLVED is not set
# BR2_PACKAGE_SYSTEMD_TIMESYNCD is not set
# BR2_PACKAGE_SYSTEMD_HOSTNAMED is not set
# BR2_PACKAGE_SYSTEMD_TIMEDATED is not set

# The post-build script: the dbus removal and its checks (os/post-build.sh, PR B41). Buildroot runs it as
# `post-build.sh TARGET_DIR` after the install. It is in the directory beside this fragment (`@here@` is that directory).
BR2_ROOTFS_POST_BUILD_SCRIPT="@here@/post-build.sh"
# DECIDED (coordinator's RULING, 2026-11-14, INPUTS "RULING (replaces ...)"): the Raze SERVES DHCP on lemnos0 from a
# per-board /29 derived from the board serial (scheme serial-hash-v1). Components/raze-base-files carries the parts:
#   files/lemnos0-address          the POSIX script that computes the board's address at boot, from /proc/device-tree/
#                                  serial-number (sh, sha256sum, cut, tr and shell arithmetic only); it writes
#                                  /run/systemd/network/10-lemnos0.network (Address=, DHCPServer=yes, DHCP=no,
#                                  LinkLocalAddressing=ipv6, IPv6AcceptRA=no, RequiredForOnline=no) and exits.
#   files/lemnos0-address.service  the oneshot that runs it, Before=systemd-networkd.service.
#   files/lemnos0-addressing.conf  the scheme's parameters (base 172.31.0.0/16, prefix 29, exclude 172.31.250.0/24).
# No fallback address: if the serial gives no subnet the script writes nothing, says why, and the unit fails.
# The board is still reached by its console (ttyGS0) and by Orion's USB link. No IPv4LL, no bridge, no dnsmasq, no
# sshd in raze-base. OPEN, not in this branch: Orion's TCP way in is to be bound to the board's IPv4 address, never a
# wildcard (the ruling). Orion's config does not carry the per-board address yet (orion.conf ssh.listen is unset).
# Dev's scheme differed on the bridge (usbbr0 over ecm+rndis) and the DHCP server (dnsmasq); see docs/prs/L2b.md.

# ---- Stripping (PR B16). Gaia's OS config starts from allnoconfig, where a bool's default does not
# apply: Buildroot's BR2_STRIP_strip (default y, depends on BR2_BINFMT_ELF) is OFF unless set here.
# It strips the target's binaries and libraries, and it also sets the kernel modules' install strip
# (linux.mk: INSTALL_MOD_STRIP=1 follows BR2_STRIP_strip). BR2_BINFMT_ELF is written out too: the
# ELF format is a choice whose default follows the MMU, so it is not left to that default.
BR2_BINFMT_ELF=y
BR2_STRIP_strip=y

# ---- Device tree overlay symbols (PR B16). The dtbs are built with symbols (dtc -@, Buildroot's DTC_FLAGS=-@),
# so an overlay can name the board's nodes. Off in allnoconfig; set here. Measured on the ZERO 3E gadget dtb:
# 55,678 -> 115,100 bytes with symbols (host dtc 1.8.1).
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DTB_OVERLAY_SUPPORT=y

# ---- Host tools the slot-boot image is made with (L2d). Gaia makes the slot's files with the BUILD's own tools, which
# this image's Buildroot builds for the host: dtc and fdtoverlay (the overlay compile and its check, `gaia.devicetree.overlay`),
# mkfs.erofs (the root, `gaia.fs.erofs`, 1.8.10 at this pin) and veritysetup (`gaia.verity`). Gaia refuses a slot image
# without them, by name (crates/nodes/verity/src/lib.rs:82, crates/core/graph/src/wire.rs:998-999). Buildroot's host
# mkfs.erofs writes no zstd: the image's compression is lzma (product settings), and the Raze kernel keeps the decompressor.
BR2_PACKAGE_HOST_DTC=y
BR2_PACKAGE_HOST_EROFS_UTILS=y
BR2_PACKAGE_HOST_CRYPTSETUP=y
